{
  "delta_from_prior": "Replaced Neon87's 16-variable asymmetric construction (which peaked at 2 because its 5-variable right side collapsed under IALH-5/DP) and Neon58's 20-variable disjoint-pair product quotient (4K2 x 4K2, which has link-twins and collapses to peak 2 under paired branching order) with a 22-variable, 344-clause XOR-lifted C7 x C7 bipartite vertex-cover/edge-transversal contradiction. It compresses cleanly to a 14-variable, 63-clause twin-free, non-product quotient with composed core UNKNOWN on both original and quotient, predicted peak 6 at layer 3 (and 4 states surviving through layers 4-6 via width-6 DP resolvents hidden from IALH-5).",
  "explanation": "### 1. Code Audit of Pinned v5 (`blq-composed-core-scc-emra-ialh5-v5`)\nAuditing the supplied v5 solver, `compression.py`, `core_refutation.py`, and pinned dependencies (`check_scc_and_growth.py`, `test_elim.py`, `test_mra.py`) reveals no soundness defect (no wrong SAT answer):\n- **Compression (`compression.py`)**: `_verify_certificate` maps all original variables densely to `1..n`, partitions all normalized original clauses by exact support (no block can be omitted because `len(counts) == len(blocks)` and `sum(counts) == len(quotient)`), verifies every form referenced by a quotient block is supported inside that block (`forms[i] & ~mask == 0`), checks local truth-table identity over all `2^|support|` local assignments, and checks `P * L = I` entrywise over GF(2) (`parity(form & column) == int(i == j)`). Surjectivity of `P` and blockwise conjunction establish equisatisfiability without trusting stabilizer discovery or greedy basis selection.\n- **Composed Core Refutation (`core_refutation.py`, `run_counting`)**: `probe` checks `set(core).issubset(set(f))` independently, compresses `core` through `compress_root`, and returns `UNSAT` only when the verified quotient is 2-SAT and `two_sat(q)` is False. Any failure, SAT quotient, or non-binary quotient returns `UNKNOWN` and falls back to exact search. Running `probe` once on an accepted exact quotient preserves soundness by equisatisfiability.\n- **Residual Solver (`run_counting_core`, `refute`, `canon_plus`, `elim_plus`, `prune`)**: IALH-5 independently rebuilds UP tables in `verify_certificate` and checks the rational dual certificate with `Fraction` arithmetic. `signed_scc`, `elim`, and `prune` (`below(g, h)` drops stronger states `g -> h`) preserve equisatisfiability.\n- **Prior Receipts Acknowledged**: Neon54/55 K6 near-parity fixtures peaked at 0 (refuted at root by globally combined IALH-5 rows), and Neon87 (16 vars, 136 clauses) compressed cleanly but peaked at 2 because its 5-variable right side fell within IALH-5/DP reach.\n\n### 2. New Growth Fixture: 22-Variable Lifted `C7 x C7` Bipartite Edge-Transversal Contradiction\nThis fixture attacks both v5's residual solver and Fable73's proposed link-twin contraction / product-structure repairs, while cleanly passing `compress_root` (not a skip) and returning `UNKNOWN` on composed core refutation.\n\n#### Quotient Structure (14 variables, 63 clauses, all width 4)\nTake two vertex-disjoint 7-cycles:\n- **Left `C7`**: `q1 - q7 - q3 - q9 - q5 - q11 - q13 - q1`\n- **Right `C7`**: `q2 - q8 - q4 - q10 - q6 - q12 - q14 - q2`\n\nThe 63 quotient clauses are:\n1. **Left negative clauses (7 clauses, width 4)**: For each of the 7 minimal vertex covers (MVCs) of Left `C7` (complements of its 7 maximal independent sets of size 3), include the all-negative 4-clause. Because every independent set of `C7` extends to a 3-MIS, satisfying all 7 negative clauses forces the 1-variables on the Left not to be a vertex cover, so at least one Left edge `(u, v)` has `u = v = 0`.\n2. **Right negative clauses (7 clauses, width 4)**: Symmetrically for the 7 MVCs of Right `C7`, forcing at least one Right edge `(w1, w2)` to have `w1 = w2 = 0`.\n3. **Positive cross clauses (49 clauses, width 4)**: For every Left edge `eL` and Right edge `eR`, include `(eL v eR)`. This forbids any simultaneously all-0 Left edge and all-0 Right edge, making the formula **UNSAT**.\n\n#### Exact Lifting to 22 Original Variables (344 clauses, max width 8)\n- `q1..q6` remain singletons `x1..x6` (independent sets `{q1, q3, q5}` on Left and `{q2, q4, q6}` on Right).\n- `q7..q14` are lifted to disjoint XOR pairs: `q7 = x7^x8`, `q8 = x9^x10`, `q9 = x11^x12`, `q10 = x13^x14`, `q11 = x15^x16`, `q12 = x17^x18`, `q13 = x19^x20`, `q14 = x21^x22`.\n- Each positive literal `+z` (`z = a^b`) expands to `[a, b]` and `[-a, -b]`; each negative literal `-z` expands to `[-a, b]` and `[a, -b]`.\n- Clause count: 44 Left negative + 44 Right negative + 256 positive cross = **344 clauses** (<= 512), max block width **8** (<= 8), 84 distinct blocks, 5504 total local space points (<= 16384).\n\n### 3. Why Compression Succeeds, Core Returns UNKNOWN, and Residual Search Grows\n1. **Exact Compression (`status='compressed'`)**: Every original block is an exact coset of the flip-stabilizer of its lifted XOR pairs, so its annihilator is the span of its singleton and XOR-pair basis forms. By `C7` symmetry, every one of the 14 quotient forms appears in exactly 18 blocks (14 positive + 4 negative), whereas any composite form appears in at most 9 blocks. Sorting by `(-freq, popcount, bitmask)` selects `x1..x6` (`popcount=1`) as quotient IDs `1..6` and the 8 XOR pairs (`popcount=2`) as quotient IDs `7..14` in exact ascending order, with compatible greedy basis and valid right inverse.\n2. **Composed Core Refutation (`UNKNOWN` at both stages)**: In the original 344-clause formula, every block's forbidden set `z` is already a full coset of its stabilizer `W`, so `counts[v] = |z|` for `v in W` and `0` otherwise; `probe` keeps all clauses (`no_clauses_removed`). In the 63-clause quotient, every block has size 1, so `probe` again returns `no_clauses_removed`.\n3. **Challenge to Fable73's Proposals**: Unlike Neon58 (`4K2 x 4K2`), `C7` is connected and completely **twin-free** (no two variables share identical incidence in cross or negative clauses), and its 7 MVCs do not factor as a Cartesian product. Fable73's link-twin contraction performs zero contractions here.\n4. **Residual Search Trajectory (Peak >= 6, Fast Termination)**:\n   - At root and layers 1-2, all clauses have width >= 3, every 5-window has > 16 UP-surviving tuples (forcing trivial integer hull `[]`), `signed_scc` finds no cycles, and `elim` is stuck (`pos * neg > pos + neg`). Thus layers 1 and 2 retain 2 and 4 states.\n   - At layer 3 (`v=3`), on the Left, `(1=0, 3=0)` is pruned by `(1=1, 3=0)` after DP eliminates `{5, 11}`, leaving 3 pairwise-incomparable Left states `(1=0, 3=1)` (vars `{7,11,13}`), `(1=1, 3=0)` (vars `{7,9,13}`), and `(1=1, 3=1)` (vars `{5,11}`), crossed with 2 incomparable Right states (`2=0`, `2=1`): **6 retained states (`peak = 6`)**.\n   - At layer 4 (`v=4`), the 5 states involving `(1=1,3=1)` or `(2=1,4=1)` drop to <= 5 variables and are refuted by IALH-5, while the 4 states `{ (1=0,3=1), (1=1,3=0) } x { (2=0,4=1), (2=1,4=0) }` retain 6 variables and 16 clauses each (including 4 width-6 DP resolvents invisible to IALH-5 `k=5`; the remaining 12 clauses of width <= 5 are simultaneously satisfied by setting the 4 surviving positive-binary endpoints to 1).\n   - Because `5` and `6` are already eliminated in those 4 states, layers 5-6 hit the `step` cache and layer 7 refutes all branches cleanly, keeping total runtime small (avoiding timeout).\n   - This is a finite growth witness (`peak >= 4`) on pinned v5, not an asymptotic lower bound.",
